1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,770
In 1890, London picked a tower
to beat the Eiffel Tower.

2
00:00:05,310 --> 00:00:07,190
This is as far as it got.

3
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:12,570
And where it stood is now one of the most
famous football pitches in the world.

4
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:16,370
The strange thing is,
the problem was written

5
00:00:16,390 --> 00:00:18,730
into the very first line of the brief.

6
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:25,970
In 1889, Paris opened the
Eiffel Tower, and it made money.

7
00:00:26,550 --> 00:00:30,310
The people behind London's tower
printed the figure themselves:

8
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:33,970
during the Paris exhibition
alone, they claimed,

9
00:00:34,130 --> 00:00:37,070
it took almost as much
as it cost to build.

10
00:00:37,630 --> 00:00:40,090
One man in London took that personally.

11
00:00:41,130 --> 00:00:43,190
Sir Edward Watkin ran railways.

12
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:44,990
He chaired the Metropolitan,

13
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:49,270
the line out of Baker Street,
along with several others.

14
00:00:50,330 --> 00:00:55,190
And he thought big. He wanted trains
running from Manchester to Paris.

15
00:00:55,650 --> 00:00:59,170
In the early 1880s he actually
started boring a tunnel

16
00:00:59,190 --> 00:01:02,130
under the Channel, until
the government stopped him.

17
00:01:02,930 --> 00:01:07,090
In 1889 he bought a country
estate at Wembley Park,

18
00:01:07,130 --> 00:01:09,750
about 12 minutes from
Baker Street by train.

19
00:01:10,330 --> 00:01:14,710
His plan was simple. Build something
people would cross England to see,

20
00:01:14,890 --> 00:01:17,170
and sell them the ticket to get there.

21
00:01:17,810 --> 00:01:20,510
That something would be a tower.

22
00:01:20,770 --> 00:01:22,270
Taller than Eiffel's.

23
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:28,990
On the 1st of November 1889,
Watkin's company published a brief.

24
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:32,630
A tower of not less than 1,200 feet.

25
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:35,950
500 guineas for the best design.

26
00:01:36,370 --> 00:01:39,710
And clause one, quote: "The designer will

27
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:43,730
assume that the foundations
are perfect." End quote.

28
00:01:44,210 --> 00:01:45,770
Remember that line.

29
00:01:46,625 --> 00:01:51,890
68 designs came in, from
Britain, America, Germany,

30
00:01:52,110 --> 00:01:57,890
Australia, Sweden, Italy,
Austria, Turkey and Canada.

31
00:01:58,570 --> 00:02:02,070
Someone published the lot as
a catalogue, and it survives.

32
00:02:02,670 --> 00:02:05,870
That's where nearly every
picture in this part comes from.

33
00:02:06,770 --> 00:02:08,230
Some of them are wonderful.

34
00:02:08,290 --> 00:02:13,650
Number 18 had a spiral road two and a
half miles long winding up the outside,

35
00:02:13,670 --> 00:02:17,050
a railway beneath it, and, I'm quoting,

36
00:02:17,510 --> 00:02:20,430
"a captive parachute
to hold four persons."

37
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:30,650
Number 29, from an engineer in Rouen
named Brunel, was 2,296 feet tall.

38
00:02:30,670 --> 00:02:35,330
Made of granite. Nearly
200,000 tons of it.

39
00:02:35,790 --> 00:02:40,030
Number 50 was Gothic, with
a steam railway spiralling

40
00:02:40,050 --> 00:02:43,910
up the inside: first,
second and third class.

41
00:02:44,870 --> 00:02:49,870
And one entrant wanted lifts big enough
to carry a carriage and a pair of horses.

42
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:53,450
My favourite is number 38.

43
00:02:53,850 --> 00:02:59,250
2,000 feet, Egyptian, with a scale
model of the Great Pyramid on the top.

44
00:02:59,930 --> 00:03:07,610
The catalogue calls it "an aerial colony":
gardens, a theatre, a museum, a temple,

45
00:03:08,170 --> 00:03:12,050
and mansions up in the
clean air, renting, it says,

46
00:03:12,070 --> 00:03:15,790
at a price "proportionate
to their Alpine altitude."

47
00:03:16,550 --> 00:03:22,150
The winner was number 37, by a London
firm: Stewart, MacLaren and Dunn.

48
00:03:22,630 --> 00:03:30,250
1,200 feet, eight legs, with a great
hall and a 90-bedroom hotel 200 feet up.

49
00:03:31,090 --> 00:03:33,750
Estimated cost: £352,000.

50
00:03:36,010 --> 00:03:38,850
To put that in today's terms:
it would have been taller

51
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:42,670
than the Eiffel Tower is now,
and taller than the Shard.

52
00:03:43,170 --> 00:03:47,650
Then came the savings. To cut
costs, the eight legs became four.

53
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:50,110
The tower that actually went up looked,

54
00:03:50,130 --> 00:03:54,870
as Brent's local historian puts
it, "a lot like Monsieur Eiffel's."

55
00:03:55,890 --> 00:04:01,870
Work started in 1892. The Engineer
magazine covered it the following autumn.

56
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:08,890
In May 1894 the pleasure grounds
around it opened: a boating lake,

57
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:12,190
sports fields, a station
built to bring the crowds.

58
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:18,090
That same spring, Watkin stepped down
from the Metropolitan after a stroke.

59
00:04:18,470 --> 00:04:21,710
And that same May, about 200 miles north,

60
00:04:22,430 --> 00:04:25,230
Blackpool opened its own
copy of the Eiffel Tower.

61
00:04:26,290 --> 00:04:30,630
518 feet, finished, open, paying its way.

62
00:04:30,690 --> 00:04:32,070
It's still there.

63
00:04:32,330 --> 00:04:34,730
Wembley's tower reached its first stage,

64
00:04:34,890 --> 00:04:40,070
a platform about 155 feet up, around 1895.

65
00:04:40,930 --> 00:04:43,910
It opened to the public in May 1896.

66
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:46,390
About an eighth of the way there.

67
00:04:47,490 --> 00:04:49,030
People did come.

68
00:04:49,390 --> 00:04:54,390
The local historian's figure is
120,000 visitors to the park in 1895.

69
00:04:55,070 --> 00:04:58,410
But fewer than Watkin's company had hoped.

70
00:04:58,830 --> 00:05:00,110
The money ran out.

71
00:05:00,770 --> 00:05:05,670
As the borough's historian puts it,
even before Watkin died in 1901,

72
00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:08,570
"there was no money left to carry on."

73
00:05:09,530 --> 00:05:11,050
And then there was the ground.

74
00:05:11,550 --> 00:05:14,210
The tower's feet began
to sink into the clay.

75
00:05:14,830 --> 00:05:17,470
Historians link it to
that cost-saving change:

76
00:05:17,490 --> 00:05:20,830
four legs instead of eight,
each carrying more weight.

77
00:05:21,350 --> 00:05:23,530
Assume the foundations are perfect.

78
00:05:24,230 --> 00:05:29,790
By 1902 the lifts were judged
unsafe and the platform shut.

79
00:05:30,530 --> 00:05:33,450
By then it already had a
nickname: Watkin's Folly.

80
00:05:33,990 --> 00:05:36,250
Taking it down started in 1904.

81
00:05:36,950 --> 00:05:40,710
In September 1907, they blew up
the foundations with dynamite.

82
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:44,690
15 years later, builders came
back to the same hilltop.

83
00:05:45,330 --> 00:05:49,130
In this aerial photo from 1922
you can see what they found:

84
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:53,190
the craters where the
tower's feet had been.

85
00:05:53,310 --> 00:05:56,110
Around them, a new
stadium was taking shape.

86
00:05:56,450 --> 00:05:59,950
It opened in 1923 as the Empire Stadium.

87
00:06:00,530 --> 00:06:01,990
You know it as Wembley.

88
00:06:02,630 --> 00:06:07,410
The very first match played
there was the 1923 FA Cup Final.

89
00:06:08,210 --> 00:06:10,870
One of the teams was West Ham United.

90
00:06:11,450 --> 00:06:16,877
They started out as the works
team of Thames Iron Works,

91
00:06:17,030 --> 00:06:18,990
the shipyard run by Arnold Hills.

92
00:06:19,890 --> 00:06:22,910
Pyramid-on-top Hills. Number 38.

93
00:06:23,810 --> 00:06:27,830
The old stadium was
demolished in 2002 and 2003.

94
00:06:28,210 --> 00:06:30,710
When the builders lowered
the pitch for the new one,

95
00:06:30,730 --> 00:06:32,790
they reportedly hit old concrete.

96
00:06:33,210 --> 00:06:35,550
It was the foundations of Watkin's tower.

97
00:06:36,190 --> 00:06:40,570
Today the tallest thing at
Wembley is the arch: 133 metres,

98
00:06:40,590 --> 00:06:43,510
a little over a third of
the tower Watkin wanted.

99
00:06:44,230 --> 00:06:50,370
The brief told 68 designers to assume
the ground was perfect. It wasn't.

100
00:06:50,690 --> 00:06:54,130
The tower sank, the money ran
out, and the spot where it

101
00:06:54,150 --> 00:06:57,490
failed became one of the most
famous pitches in the world.

102
00:06:58,070 --> 00:07:00,190
I'm Eric Hoffman, and this was UNBUILT.

103
00:07:00,750 --> 00:07:04,690
Every picture in this video is real,
and the sources are in the description.

